{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_xsvnitzc5sbzeywnnrimskohpiux5hvrmxluw5yxjfgdgixvmlhq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_0143xt93jk51pon3F4f2n2d4","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Audit C vs Rust for divergences","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"You are auditing a C-to-Rust translation for behavioural divergence. DO NOT MODIFY ANY FILES. Report findings only.\n\nFiles (read them fully, they are small):\n- C ground truth: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-X6YTtt/c_src/src/lib.c (219 lines)\n- Rust translation: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-X6YTtt/translation/src/lib.rs\n\nThe C library has ONE public symbol, `int jumpnode(int operation_mode, int node_id, int depth, int flags)`. Everything else is `static`. Note `initialize_test_data` is static AND never called, so in the shipped .so `node_count` is permanently 0.\n\nDifferential tests already PASS for: all four switch arms, the default arm, all error sentinels (18/34/66/130/2), safe_double_to_int clamps (incl. ULP sweeps around both thresholds), compute_size_metric, process_backward (incl. negative start_offset via a mid-buffer pointer), add_node capacity, find_node_by_id, mode 3 densely swept over node_id x depth in -600..600 and all power-of-ten neighbourhoods, and ~200k randomized calls.\n\nYour job: find any input or condition where the Rust could STILL differ from the C, that the above would NOT catch. Focus specifically and only on:\n\n1. `c_sprintf_node_depth` + `fmt_int` in the Rust vs `sprintf(buffer, \"Node_%d_Depth_%d\", node_id, depth)` in C. Check the buffer-size math (C buffer is `char buffer[50]`), the i32::MIN rendering, and whether `fmt_int`'s `bytes: [u8; 12]` array can ever overflow (it writes digits then possibly a '-'; count the max bytes written for i32::MIN). Report the exact worst-case index written.\n2. `safe_double_to_int`: C does `(int)value` after clamping. Compare with Rust's `value as c_int` (which saturates instead of being UB). Identify any double input where C's clamped `(int)` cast and Rust's `as` differ. Consider NaN specifically and state whether NaN is reachable from any public call.\n3. Any place the C relies on `int` overflow / implicit conversion where the Rust uses a different width or a checked/saturating op. In particular compare C's `for (i = 0; i < depth && ...; i++)` loop against the Rust `while` loop translation — check whether `i` can overflow and whether the increment placement relative to `break` matches.\n4. `process_backward`: C compares `ptr > start` where both are `int*`. Rust uses `array.offset(size as isize)` and `array.offset(start_offset as isize)`. Check for any size/start_offset value where `size as isize` or `start_offset as isize` behaves differently from C's pointer arithmetic (note size is `size_t`, start_offset is `int`).\n5. `find_node_by_id` / mode 4's backward walk: compare the pointer comparison `iter > node_storage` and the `NODE_COUNT as isize` offsets for negative or huge NODE_COUNT.\n\nFor each item report: DIVERGENCE (with the exact triggering input) or MATCHES (with a one-line reason). Be concrete and terse. Do not speculate about UB the tests already exclude (negative depth in mode 2 reading out of bounds is known and accepted)."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":426,"cache_read_input_tokens":125749,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":426,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4eff9d63-9e14-4627-9f0a-2df67eb432d6","uuid":"570ec122-1994-425a-811d-f4604288aff2","timestamp":"2026-09-05T14:55:03.374Z"}
