{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01LDGidkxxxXgo4TVTUGoYRh","type":"tool_result","content":"    Finished `release` profile [optimized] target(s) in 0.01s\n52\n--- assertion sites ---\ntests/phase_b_s1.rs:6 tests/phase_b_s2.rs:31 tests/phase_d_exhaustive.rs:12 tests/phase_c_errors.rs:72","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4eff9d63-9e14-4627-9f0a-2df67eb432d6","uuid":"04a0238c-7c24-4b77-b932-7a26f8595ca5","timestamp":"2026-09-05T14:57:36.917Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"    Finished `release` profile [optimized] target(s) in 0.01s\n52\n--- assertion sites ---\ntests/phase_b_s1.rs:6 tests/phase_b_s2.rs:31 tests/phase_d_exhaustive.rs:12 tests/phase_c_errors.rs:72","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_mmhmqdtwlsikele5gyzrmbhssyi6ehqj3htx6momajwdncx7e6lq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. **All four phases pass; the translation is correct.**\n\n## Results\n\n| Gate | Status |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` — symbol parity | ✅ C exports exactly `{jumpnode}`; Rust default build is symbol-identical. `comm -23` empty in all 4 configs. 0 undefined non-libc symbols. |\n| Phase B — all 25 `CONFIGS.md` rows | ✅ randomized (fixed seed) + dense sweeps |\n| Binary stdout comparison | N/A — `CMakeLists.txt` builds only `SHARED`; crate is `cdylib`-only, no `[[bin]]` |\n| Phase C — all 8 `ERRORS.md` rows | ✅ each asserts the *specific* sentinel (18/34/66/130/2), not just \"failed\" |\n| Every feature combo | ✅ `{}` and `{expose_init_test_data}` × release **and** debug (debug has `overflow-checks=on`, proving no hidden non-wrapping arithmetic) |\n\n52 tests, ~120 assertion sites, run via `./verify.sh` (builds C, builds harness, checks symbols, runs all combos).\n\n## The key finding in Phase A\n\n`initialize_test_data` is `static` **and never called**, so the shipped `.so` has `node_count == 0` permanently — modes 1/2/4 are pure error paths and only mode 3 computes. Testing just the public `.so` would have left `find_node_by_id`, `add_node`, `process_backward`, `safe_double_to_int` and mode 4's backward walk **completely unverified**.\n\nI closed that gap without touching `c_src/`: `tests/c_harness/harness.c` `#include`s `c_src/src/lib.c` verbatim and exports hooks whose names match the Rust crate's `expose_init_test_data` hooks, so both sides are driven identically through `libloading`. (I added the extra Rust hooks; all are feature-gated, so the default build stays symbol-identical to C.)\n\n## Two real bugs found — both in my own test rig, not the translation\n\n1. **Shared-state race.** `dlopen` of the same path returns the same handle, so `node_count` is shared across test threads. Concurrent tests interleaved mutations between the C call and the Rust call, producing a *false* divergence (`jumpnode(4,6,…)` → C 66 vs Rust −2147483566). Fixed with a `STATE_LOCK` held by every test in a binary.\n2. **Stale `.so`.** `cargo test` does *not* rebuild the `cdylib`, so tests were silently loading an old artifact. I caught this via a negative control — injecting two bugs (`1.5`→`1.4`, `+010`→`+011`) and finding tests still passed. `verify.sh` now always `cargo build`s first. Both injected bugs are correctly caught with exact inputs.\n\n## One documented divergence, unreachable\n\n`safe_double_to_int(NaN)`: C returns `-2147483648` (x86 `cvttsd2si`), Rust `as` returns `0`. C's `(int)NaN` is **UB**. Unreachable from the public API — `add_node` hardcodes `data[] = {0100,0200,0300,0400}` so `sqrt` never gets a negative argument, and mode 1 only sums finite values. I left the Rust as a faithful non-UB translation rather than hard-coding a platform-specific UB result, and added a test asserting unreachability. Recorded as row U3 in `ERRORS.md`, alongside two other excluded-UB rows (mode 2's negative-`depth` out-of-bounds read; `16*flags` signed overflow, which I pin down separately since both toolchains emit a wrapping multiply).\n\nThe only edit to `src/lib.rs` was adding feature-gated test hooks — the translated logic needed no fixes."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":460,"cache_read_input_tokens":149664,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":460,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4eff9d63-9e14-4627-9f0a-2df67eb432d6","uuid":"4809a715-1345-465c-af1a-cca7ea560907","timestamp":"2026-09-05T14:57:55.121Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_cicnvkodnnsvo3ttxxcby2xruwixv3ozv57cufthrnsge2oqq2oq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1915,"cache_read_input_tokens":39481,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1915,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_0143xt93jk51pon3F4f2n2d4","session_id":"4eff9d63-9e14-4627-9f0a-2df67eb432d6","uuid":"b6d18a26-926c-403d-b92b-64182929026a","timestamp":"2026-09-05T14:58:01.920Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Audit C vs Rust for divergences"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_cicnvkodnnsvo3ttxxcby2xruwixv3ozv57cufthrnsge2oqq2oq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read both files fully and verified the interesting cases empirically (gcc -O2 x86-64 vs rustc).\n\n## 1. `c_sprintf_node_depth` + `fmt_int` — MATCHES (buffer math is sound)\n\n- `fmt_int` worst case is `v = i32::MIN`: 10 digit bytes go to `tmp[0..=9]`, then `'-'` to `tmp[10]`, `n = 11`. **Exact worst-case index written = 10** in both `tmp` and `bytes` (`[u8; 12]`), leaving 1 byte of slack. No overflow is possible for any `c_int`.\n- Magnitude for `i32::MIN` is computed as `(v as i64).unsigned_abs() as u32` = 2147483648, so it renders `\"-2147483648\"` exactly like `%d`. Verified `fmt_int` lengths: 11 for `i32::MIN`, 10 for `i32::MAX`.\n- Buffer: worst case `5 + 11 + 7 + 11 = 34` chars at `buffer[0..=33]`, NUL at `buffer[34]` — max index written 34 of 50. C confirmed identical: `sprintf` produces `\"Node_-2147483648_Depth_-2147483648\"`, `strlen == 34`, so `compute_size_metric` = 76 on both sides. No overflow in either language, and the largest reachable mode-3 result is `76 + 127 = 203`.\n\n## 2. `safe_double_to_int` — DIVERGENCE on NaN (reachable through `jumpnode` only when the store is populated, i.e. in the harness, not in the shipped .so)\n\nConfirmed on x86-64:\n\n| input | C `(int)value` | Rust `value as c_int` |\n|---|---|---|\n| NaN | **-2147483648** (cvttsd2si indefinite) | **0** (saturating cast) |\n| ±inf | 2147483647 / -2147483648 | same (clamped first) |\n| everything else | truncate-toward-zero | identical |\n\nNaN survives both clamp comparisons (`NaN > x` and `NaN < x` are both false), so it reaches the cast unclamped. This is the one real value-level divergence in the whole file. Note it is x86-64-specific: on aarch64 `fcvtzs` yields 0 and C would match.\n\nReachability:\n- Shipped `.so`: **not reachable.** `NODE_COUNT ≡ 0`, so modes 1 and 4 both return their sentinels (18 / 66) before any `safe_double_to_int` call. Mode 3 never calls it.\n- Differential harness (`expose_init_test_data`): **reachable through the public `jumpnode` symbol.** Two concrete triggers:\n  - `jumpnode_test_add_node(1, -1, f64::NAN)` then `jumpnode(1, 1, 0, 0)` → C `-2147483648`, Rust `0`.\n  - No-NaN-input variant: `add_node(1, 2, INFINITY); add_node(2, -1, -INFINITY);` then `jumpnode(1, 1, 1, 0)` → `inf + (-inf * 1.5) = NaN` → C `-2147483648`, Rust `0`. Same divergence in mode 4's `backward_sum` for a node whose `value` is NaN with `NODE_COUNT > 2`.\n  - Also directly via `jumpnode_test_safe_double_to_int(f64::NAN)`.\n\nNaN cannot arise from `sqrt` in mode 4 (`add_node` hardcodes positive `data[]`, zeroed storage gives `sqrt(0)`), so the `value` field is the only injection point.\n\n## 3. Integer width / overflow / loop-increment placement — MATCHES\n\n- Mode 1 loop: increment placement is faithful — `i += 1` is the last statement of the `while` body and the `break` on `parent_node == NULL` precedes it, exactly like `for(...; i++)` where `break` skips the increment. `i` cannot overflow: the guard `i < depth` bounds `i` by `depth ≤ i32::MAX` after the increment, so `i += 1` never wraps (and never panics in debug).\n- Mode 2 loops: `i < 4` and `i = 4..0o20`; `i.wrapping_mul(0o7)` can't wrap for `i < 16`, and indices stay inside `temp_array[20]`.\n- Mode 4 loop: `while i < 3 && iter > base` with trailing `i += 1`, no `break` — structurally identical to the C `for`.\n- `compute_size_metric`: `len as c_int` matches gcc's implementation-defined `(int)len` truncation; `wrapping_mul(2).wrapping_add(0o10)` matches gcc's actual wrapping codegen (would need a ≥1 GiB string via the test hook to even reach the C UB).\n- `(array_size as c_int).wrapping_mul(flags)`: verified gcc -O2 wraps identically, e.g. `flags = 2000000000` → both give `1935229064`, `flags = -2000000000` → both `-1935228792`.\n- `sum.wrapping_add(*ptr)` in `process_backward`: only overflowable via the accepted out-of-bounds negative-`depth` read; gcc wraps too.\n\n## 4. `process_backward` pointer arithmetic — MATCHES for every `jumpnode`-reachable input; harness-only abort for `size ≥ 2^61`\n\n- `start_offset as isize` sign-extends exactly like C's `int → ptrdiff_t`. Identical for all values, including `INT_MIN` (verified: both compute `array - 8 GiB`, both then walk 2^31 elements and die the same way — no Rust UB-check fires because the address doesn't wrap).\n- `size as isize` preserves the bit pattern, so the resulting byte address (`± size*4 mod 2^64`) is bit-identical to C's `array + size` for **all** `size_t` values. Verified `size = usize::MAX`: both produce `array - 4` bytes and both return 0.\n- Only difference found: Rust's optional `ptr::offset` precondition check. With `-C debug-assertions=on`, `size = 1<<62` (or `(1<<63)+8`) **aborts** with `\"ptr::offset requires the address calculation to not overflow\"`, while C silently computes the wrapped pointer (`ptr == array`, resp. `ptr == array+8`, returning 0 resp. a sum). Not reachable from `jumpnode` (`array_size` is hardcoded `0o20`); reachable only via `jumpnode_test_process_backward`, and only in debug-assertions builds. Both sides are UB, so this is a build-config artifact rather than a semantic error, but it will make a debug-built harness abort where C returns a value.\n\n## 5. `find_node_by_id` / mode 4 backward walk — MATCHES; but a related DIVERGENCE in `add_node`\n\n- `find_node_by_id`: `while i < count` with both operands `c_int` matches C's signed compare, so negative `NODE_COUNT` yields 0 iterations and NULL on both sides (mode 1/2/4 then return 18/34/66 identically).\n- Mode 4: the `NODE_COUNT > 2` guard excludes negatives in both. For huge counts (up to `INT_MAX`), `base.offset(NODE_COUNT as isize)` = 32 · 2147483647 ≈ 64 GiB, which fits `isize` and can never wrap a 64-bit address, so no UB-check fires and the pointer equals C's `&node_storage[node_count]` bit-for-bit. `iter > base` is the same unsigned address compare as C's `iter > node_storage`, and the three decrements read the identical addresses. No divergence.\n- **DIVERGENCE (outside the listed items, same root cause — negative `NODE_COUNT`):** `add_node`'s capacity guard changed width. C is `node_count >= MAX_NODES` (signed `int` compare); Rust is `NODE_COUNT as usize >= MAX_NODES`. With `NODE_COUNT < 0`, C's guard is false — it proceeds to write `node_storage[negative]` and returns `STATUS_OK (0)` — while Rust's cast makes the guard true and returns `STATUS_ERROR (2)`. Trigger: `jumpnode_test_set_node_count(-1); jumpnode_test_add_node(1, -1, 1.0);` → C `0`, Rust `2`, and `NODE_COUNT` afterwards is `0` in C vs `-1` in Rust. Harness-only (C side is UB), but it is an observable return-value difference that the \"add_node capacity\" tests would miss if they only sweep non-negative counts.\n\n### Summary\n- Real value divergence: **NaN in `safe_double_to_int`** (C `-2147483648` vs Rust `0` on x86-64), reachable through `jumpnode` whenever a node's `value` is NaN or produces `inf + (-inf)`.\n- Width-change divergence: **`add_node`'s `as usize` capacity guard** for negative `NODE_COUNT`.\n- Everything else in the five focus areas matches, including the `fmt_int`/buffer math (worst index 10 of 12, and 34 of 50), the loop-increment placement, and all `as isize` pointer offsets.\n\nFiles: `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-X6YTtt/c_src/src/lib.c`, `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-X6YTtt/translation/src/lib.rs` (relevant lines: `safe_double_to_int` 145-156, `add_node` guard 84, `fmt_int` 313-347, `c_sprintf_node_depth` 280-299, `process_backward` 108-121, mode 4 walk 252-267). No files were modified."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1915,"cache_read_input_tokens":39481,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1915,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_0143xt93jk51pon3F4f2n2d4","session_id":"4eff9d63-9e14-4627-9f0a-2df67eb432d6","uuid":"5da7fac9-dddb-4876-b2d6-06695df11f9e","timestamp":"2026-09-05T14:58:41.407Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Audit C vs Rust for divergences"}
